  load-exchanges: venues
  load-symbols: instruments
  load-sessions: 7,560 business days
  load-bars: OHLCV, one row per (symbol, session)
  load-stats: derived features, one row per bar
  load-models: 8 strategies
  backtest-read: 3-relation join, one cross-section per rebalance
  result-insert: 8 rows per rebalance, one per model
  backtest-replay: the same cross-sections re-read, x1
  compare-all: every result row, joined to its model
  compare-one: one model's history joined to its model, x4
  read-bar-lookup: pk equality on daily_bars
  read-bar-range: pk BETWEEN on daily_bars, 200 wide
  read-symbol-history: seqscan, one symbol's whole run
  read-day-slice: seqscan, one session's features
  read-join-point: bar -> symbol -> exchange, by pk
  read-join-exists: semi-join: symbols with a bar
  agg-global: whole-relation fold, no GROUP BY
  agg-by-symbol: GROUP BY symbol_id - 8 groups
  agg-by-session: GROUP BY session_no - one per day
  agg-day-slice: one session's cross section, grouped
  agg-distinct: COUNT(DISTINCT symbol_id) over the run

  ingest: 60,480 bars + 60,480 feature rows, batched 200 per transaction
  backtest-read is the same 3-relation join the ckdbs run issues, written identically and in the same order
  daily_stats and model_results carry no index, matching the ckdbs run's HEAP relations - which is what the sweep's index column adds
  no aggregates: the model comparison is reduced client-side, because the other engine's grammar has none
  every row is decoded and formatted client-side, as the ckdbs client does, so neither side is handed a client-cost advantage

